# ==============================================================================
# pam_env.conf - PAM environment configuration for calife
#
# This module sets the environment based on variable definitions.
# It is part of the Pluggable Authentication Modules (PAM) system.
#
# Syntax:
#   varname TYPE [DEFAULT=default_value] [OVERRIDE=default_value]
#   - varname: The name of the environment variable to set.
#   - TYPE: 'ENV' for environment variables, 'SYSTEM' for system variables (e.g., PATH, USER).
#   - DEFAULT: The value to use if the variable is not found in the system.
#   - OVERRIDE: The value to use regardless of the system value (use with caution).
#
# Comments are indicated by '#'. Empty lines are ignored.
# ==============================================================================

# Load standard system environment variables (like PATH, USER, HOME, SHELL, LOGNAME).
# These are typically provided by the operating system and should generally be trusted.
# If no specific variables are listed here, the default behavior is to import system
# variables. However, explicitly listing common ones ensures predictable behavior
# across different Linux distributions.
SYSTEM PATH /usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
SYSTEM HOME $HOME
SYSTEM USER $USER
SYSTEM LOGNAME $LOGNAME
SYSTEM SHELL $SHELL
# PAGER is often used by interactive tools; set a sensible default.
SYSTEM PAGER less
# TERM is crucial for terminal-based applications to render correctly.
SYSTEM TERM $TERM
# LANG or LC_ALL determine locale settings for text processing.
# If not set by the caller, default to C (POSIX) for maximum compatibility.
SYSTEM LANG C
SYSTEM LC_ALL C
# LANGUAGE is used by GNU gettext for translation fallback.
SYSTEM LANGUAGE C

# ==============================================================================
# Optional: Define application-specific defaults for the 'calife' service.
# These ensure that even if the user's session environment is incomplete,
# critical service variables are set.
# ==============================================================================

# Example: Set a default configuration directory if not provided by PAM stack.
# ENV CALIFE_CONFIG_DIR /etc/calife

# Example: Set the home directory for service operations if not inherited.
# ENV CALIFE_HOME $HOME